A global scalar loads at its own width -- and an array sized by its
initialiser must say that it is an array.

TWO CHANGES, because the first cannot be made safely without the second.

1. THE LOAD. primary_expr_variable defaulted `size` to register_size for
anything that is not a local or an argument. The STORE already used the type's
width, so for a global scalar the two disagreed:

    str_w0,[x1]      g = -3   four bytes
    ldr_x0,[x0]      read g   EIGHT bytes

The eight-byte read takes g and the four bytes after it. A positive value
survives because the neighbours are zero -- which is why every global test
written with positive values passed, and why this site was examined and cleared
twice before -- and a negative one does not: -3 stores as 0xFFFFFFFD and reads
back as 0x????????FFFFFFFD.

    int g;
    int main(void){ g = 0 - 3; if(g != 0 - 3) return 1; return 0; }
    gcc 0, micro-c 1

WHAT IT COST. tcc compares `tok != TOK_EOF`, and TOK_EOF is (-1). The
comparison failed, so tcc_error("...") fired at tccpp.c, pp_expr was left set
at 256 instead of being reset on the next line, and error1 routed every
diagnostic to pp_error -- the handler for preprocessor EXPRESSION errors, in a
file with no directives at all. pp_error then rewinds and lexes to TOK_EOF:

    while (next(), tok != TOK_EOF)

which is the same comparison, so it never terminated. 5,316,311 iterations,
with vsnprintf at 74% of the trace because it is the most expensive thing
inside the loop. The tcc micro-c builds hung instead of compiling.

2. THE DISCRIMINATOR, which is why 1 took three attempts. register_size is
CORRECT for a global array, whose symbol is a pointer CELL rather than the
storage, so the narrowing must apply to scalars only. array_modifier could not
tell them apart:

    int g               armod=0    scalar
    char m[] = "hello"  armod=0    array   <- indistinguishable
    int a[3]            armod=3    array

global_static_array returned the DECLARED size, and an array sized by its
initialiser has none. The length was already known -- `ai`, the byte count the
string branch pads from -- it was simply not recorded. It is now, so `char m[]
= "hello"` records 6 and the symbol can say what it is.

That is a correctness fix in its own right: any site asking array_modifier to
separate an array from a scalar got the wrong answer for exactly the form C
sizes from its initialiser.

MEASURED: difftest stays 84/85 -- no regression -- negative globals, global
char arrays, global int arrays and global pointers all match gcc. mc-tcc STOPS
HANGING: `-c` returns 1 rather than timing out, and the freestanding hello
world it builds runs and prints "hello, world".

STILL BROKEN: tcc now reports `<command line>:53: error: struct/union/enum
already defined`, which is a real error to chase rather than a hang hiding one.
14-function-pointer-member still faults.


NAMED zzza, NOT aab. The workflows apply this directory by glob, so the series
runs in ALPHABETICAL order -- and `aab` sorted ahead of zzu..zzz, which is not
where it was generated or tested. It has to land after zzz.

